The Security Council,

Recalling its previous resolutions and statements of its President concerning the Democratic Republic of the Congo (DRC), including resolutions 2293 (2016), 2360 (2017), 2424 (2018), 2478 (2019), 2528 (2020), 2582 (2021), 2641 (2022), 2666 (2022), 2688 (2023), 2717 (2023), 2738 (2024), 2746 (2024), 2765 (2024), and 2773 (2025),

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity, and territorial integrity of the DRC, as well as all States in the region, and emphasizing that any violation of the territorial integrity and sovereignty of the DRC is unacceptable and must cease,

Expressing its continued deep concern at the ongoing deterioration of the security and humanitarian situation in eastern DRC, exacerbated by the ongoing offensive of the 23 March Movement (M23) with the direct support and participation of the Rwanda Defence Forces (RDF), and their continued control of key population centres and strategic areas including Goma, Bukavu, and Walikale,

Condemning in the strongest terms all human rights violations and abuses and violations of international humanitarian law committed by armed groups and security forces, including summary executions, sexual and gender-based violence, attacks on civilians and internally displaced persons sites, and the recruitment and use of children,

Expressing alarm at the critical humanitarian emergency in North Kivu and South Kivu, marked by massive civilian displacement, acute food insecurity, and restrictions on humanitarian access,

Reiterating that there can be no military solution to the crisis in eastern DRC, and reaffirming its unwavering support for regional diplomatic initiatives, particularly the Luanda process led by Angola and the Nairobi process led by the East African Community, while welcoming subsequent dialogue efforts, including the discussions facilitated in Doha,

Reiterating the critical importance of neutralizing all armed groups operating in eastern DRC, including the M23, the Forces démocratiques de libération du Rwanda (FDLR), the Allied Democratic Forces (ADF), and local armed groups (Wazalendo),

Underlining the link between the illicit exploitation and trade of natural resources, including coltan, gold, and tin, and the proliferation and trafficking of arms as one of the major factors fueling and exacerbating conflicts in the African Great Lakes region, and noting with deep concern the seizure and control of major mining sites, including the Rubaya mining area, by armed groups,

Reiterating its condemnation of all attacks against the United Nations Organization Stabilization Mission in the DRC (MONUSCO) and the Southern African Development Community Mission in the DRC (SAMIDRC), and paying tribute to the peacekeepers who have lost their lives,

Taking note of the reports of the Group of Experts on the DRC mandated by resolution 2738 (2024),

Determining that the situation in the DRC continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security in the region,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to renew until 1 July 2026 the measures as set out in paragraphs 1 to 6 of resolution 2293 (2016), including its reaffirmations and exemptions;

2. Reaffirms that the measures described in paragraph 5 of resolution 2293 (2016) shall apply to individuals and entities as designated by the Committee, in accordance with the criteria set out in resolution 2293 (2016) and subsequent resolutions, including those involved in the illicit trade and exploitation of natural resources in the DRC that support armed groups or criminal networks, and those planning, directing, or committing acts that violate international humanitarian law or human rights law;

3. Decides to extend until 1 August 2026 the mandate of the Group of Experts established pursuant to resolution 1533 (2004) and renewed by subsequent resolutions, expresses its intention to review the mandate and take appropriate action regarding further extension no later than 1 July 2026, and requests the Secretary-General to take the necessary administrative measures as expeditiously as possible to re-establish the Group of Experts, in consultation with the Committee;

4. Requests the Group of Experts to provide to the Council, after discussion with the Committee, a mid-term report no later than 30 December 2025, and a final report no later than 15 June 2026, as well as to submit monthly updates to the Committee, except in the months where the mid-term and final reports are due;

5. Demands that the M23 immediately cease all hostilities, fully and unconditionally withdraw from all captured areas, including Goma, Bukavu, Walikale, and the Rubaya mining district, and cease all attempts to establish parallel administrative structures;

6. Reiterates its demand that the Rwanda Defence Forces cease all support to the M23 and immediately and unconditionally withdraw from the territory of the DRC;

7. Condemns any provision of support to the FDLR by any party, including members of the Congolese military (FARDC), and demands the immediate cessation of such support and the full, swift implementation of commitments to dismantle and neutralize the FDLR;

8. Calls upon all parties to respect the ceasefire agreed under regional auspices, to de-escalate tensions immediately, and to engage in good faith in diplomatic dialogue through the coordinated Luanda and Nairobi processes;

9. Demands that all parties allow and facilitate full, safe, immediate, and unhindered humanitarian access to all populations in need throughout North Kivu and South Kivu, in accordance with international humanitarian law and humanitarian principles;

10. Strongly condemns the illicit exploitation and smuggling of Congolese mineral resources, particularly coltan and artisanal minerals, which finance armed groups and perpetuate regional instability, and calls upon all regional and international actors, transit hubs, and private sector entities to exercise strict due diligence in line with the OECD Due Diligence Guidance and the ICGLR Regional Certification Mechanism;

11. Demands that all armed groups and foreign forces cease targeting peacekeepers, humanitarian personnel, and civil society, and immediately halt all electronic warfare activities, including the jamming and spoofing of GPS and the deployment of surface-to-air missile systems, which endanger MONUSCO, SAMIDRC, and humanitarian air operations;

12. Reaffirms its readiness to consider further targeted sanctions against any individuals or entities that undermine peace, security, or stability in the DRC, including those violating the arms embargo, recruiting child soldiers, perpetrating sexual violence, obstructing MONUSCO or humanitarian access, or illicitly exploiting mineral wealth to fund armed actors;

13. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

